# Build products. *.exe covers goc.exe / goa.exe and every compiled example on
# Windows; Linux ELF output has no suffix and is ignored per-directory below.
*.exe
.cache/
*.out
*.o
*.s

# Toolchain binaries (goc, goa, elfcheck, msgboxcheck) all land in bin/.
# NOTE: never ignore /goc or /src at the root -- src/ is now the compiler
# source tree and would be swallowed by such a pattern.
/bin/

# src/examples/ keeps the .c sources plus whatever data files an example
# embeds; everything else there is a build product: src/examples/*.asm (goc -S
# output) and src/examples/foo (Linux ELF). Test runs use `goc -o bin/goc-out`
# so this rule is a safety net for hand-invoked `goc -S`/`goc -c` runs that write
# next to the source.
src/examples/*
!src/examples/*.c
# ...and the same for sources one level down: the multi-file regression builds
# from src/examples/multi/{a,b}.c. Negating the files is not enough on its own
# -- git does not descend into an excluded *directory*, so each such directory
# has to be re-included before the file rule can match.
!src/examples/*/
!src/examples/**/*.c
# Data files an example reads or #embeds are inputs, not build products, so they
# have to be tracked too: c23_embed.c #embeds embed_data.txt, and without it a
# fresh clone fails all six legs with "include file not found". Name the files
# explicitly rather than allowing *.txt -- the regression examples write scratch
# files like goc_io_test.txt and goc_freopen.txt, and a wildcard would commit
# those too.
!src/examples/embed_data.txt
!src/examples/*/embed_data.txt

# Same rule for goa's own examples. goa/run_tests.sh assembles each example
# where it lives: Windows .exe output is already caught by the *.exe rule above,
# but the Linux ELF output has no suffix, so name it explicitly.
goa/examples/linux/*
!goa/examples/linux/*.asm

# ...and the AT&T front end's test samples. These are LLVM AsmPrinter output
# checked in as test *inputs*, not build products -- without them the breadth
# test in goa/att_e2e_test.go skips and the front end loses its most
# valuable coverage. Only a handful are committed; tools/gen-att-samples.sh
# regenerates the full corpus on demand, and the gitignore rule is what keeps
# that regeneration from showing up as 70 untracked files.
goa/testdata/att/*
!goa/testdata/att/longmin.s
!goa/testdata/att/intprint.s
!goa/testdata/att/print_thin.s
!goa/testdata/att/variadic.s

# src/goclib/ is source, not build products. src/goc reads it from disk at run
# time and src/main.go embeds it, so in both cases the .c/.h files belong in
# the repository -- and the *.exe rule above does not reach them anyway.

# Local agent workspace, not part of the project.
.workbuddy/

# Ad-hoc build artifacts and local scratch space from debugging sessions.
goclib_out.bin
scratch/

# Go build cache written next to the project when GOCACHE points here.
.gocache/

# Test/debug scratch dirs produced by goc runs (goa work areas).
tmp/
buildtmp/

# Python bytecode caches from the QEMU/Unicorn runners.
__pycache__/

# Scratch dirs from the C89-C17 / C23 test-suite builders (probes, temp exes).
.tmp/

# C23/C89-C17 suite build dirs (dual-compiler harness scratch).
tests/*/_build/

# Perf-benchmark dir: keep only the .c sources, ignore built artifacts.
bench/*
!bench/*.c
goa/zz_*.tmpl
src/**/zz_*_test.go.tmpl

# Files the file-I/O examples write into the working directory when run by
# hand. run_tests.sh sends their output to bin/goc-out/, but a bare
# `./bin/goc.exe src/examples/io_test.c` from the repo root drops them here.
goc_io_test.txt
goc_freopen.txt
